<header class="sp-head"><a class="sp-portrait" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser.jpg" alt="" width="104" height="104"></a><p class="featured-region">26 July 1956 · Alexandria</p><h1 class="sp-title">Nasser Nationalises the Suez Canal</h1><p class="sp-by"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a></p></header>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>A week after the United States and Britain withdrew their offer to help finance the Aswan High Dam, President Gamal Abdel Nasser addressed a vast crowd in Alexandria at a rally marking four years since the revolution that had overthrown the monarchy. Midway through the speech he spoke the name of Ferdinand de Lesseps, builder of the canal: the agreed signal for Egyptian officials to take over the offices of the Suez Canal Company.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>In substance</h2><p class="sp-para">The Suez Canal Company is nationalised. Its revenues, which have gone to foreign shareholders, will now pay for the High Dam, and the canal will be run by Egyptians.</p><p class="sp-source">A summary of the announcement made in his speech at Alexandria, 26 July 1956.</p><div class="sp-full"></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>Britain and France, the company's main owners, secretly planned with Israel to retake the canal, and in October and November 1956 Israel invaded Sinai and British and French troops landed at Port Said. American and Soviet pressure forced them to withdraw, a humiliation that ended Britain's standing as the dominant power in the Middle East and made Nasser the hero of Arab nationalism. The High Dam was built with Soviet help.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-arabism">Pan-Arabism</a></div></div></section>
